# A search result shows the line it matched on, with the words lit — and that
# highlighting stays out of find's count.
#
# [APP-WORKAROUND] Painting behind a run of text means knowing where that run
# landed on screen, and nothing in the library will say (afterhours_gaps.md
# #51). The band positions are re-derived by redoing the renderer's own layout,
# the same trick src/ui/find_highlight.h uses — and now literally the same
# code, so there is one transcription of the renderer's constants rather than
# two that rot apart.
#
# The counters, though, are two, and this script is why. The find tally is only
# worth reading because it equals the bands find painted
# (find_counts_only_what_it_paints.e2e). A band painted in the SIDEBAR is not
# one of those, so it is counted separately: here both audits are on at once,
# the sidebar paints one band and find still reports exactly its two.
#
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":["t2"],"active_tab":"t2","theme":"dark"}
# env: HANABI_FIND_DEMO=ledger HANABI_FIND_AUDIT=1 HANABI_SNIPPET_AUDIT=1

# Before the sidebar is searched at all: find's tally and find's bands agree.
expect_text "1 of 2"
expect_text "bands 2"

# Search the list. The row that matches now carries the line it matched on.
click 140 58
wait_frames 4
type "workers"
wait_frames 10
expect_text "two workers still out"
expect_text "1 of 3 workers has reported"

# One occurrence in that snippet, one band painted behind it.
expect_text "snippet bands 1"

# And find's own numbers are untouched by it — same tally, same bands.
expect_text "1 of 2"
expect_text "bands 2"
